Special case e → ∞: foetal development
Foetal development differs from that in eggs in that energy reserves are supplied continuously via the placenta. The feeding and digestion processes are not involved. Otherwise, foetal development is taken to be identical to egg development, with initial reserves that can be taken to be infinitely large, for practical purposes. At birth, the neonate receives an amount of reserves from the mother, such that the reserve density of the neonate equals that of the mother. So the approximation [E] → ∞ or e → ∞ for the foetus can be made for the whole gestation period, because the foetus lives on the reserves of the mother. In other words: unlike eggs, the development of foetuses is not restricted by energy reserves. Initially the egg and foetus develop in the same way, but the foetus keeps developing at a rate not restricted by the amount of reserves till the end of the gestation time, while the development of the egg becomes retarded, due to depletion of the reserves. The special case e → ∞ makes that d
